<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Iwaz Khalji</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1227">1227</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Ghiyath al-Din Iwaz Khalji</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Iwaz Khalji, who ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Ghiyath al-Din from about 1212 to 1227, was the most capable ruler of the Khalji dynasty of Bengal. He built roads and embankments, created a river fleet and struck coins in his own name. He was defeated and killed by forces sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iltutmish">Iltutmish</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iwaz-khalji.jpg" alt="Iwaz Khalji" width="320" height="157" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Islamic_Sultanates._Bengal._Ghiyath_al-Din_%27Iwad._Governor,_AH_614-616_AD_1217-1220._Struck_in_the_name_of_Shams_al-Din_Iltutmish,_Sultan_of_Dehli.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Iwaz was born Husam ud-Din Iwaz into a Muslim family of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalaj">Khalaj</a>, a tribe of Turkic origin settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, at Garmsir; his father was Husayn Khalji. As a young man he carried goods with a donkey, and a story tells that dervishes he had fed prayed for him and told him to go to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>. He served as a deputy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakhtiyar-khalji">Bakhtiyar Khalji</a>'s advance into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and was made Jagirdar of Kangori in North Bengal. In 1208 Qaimaz Rumi, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a>, sent by Qutb al-Din Aibak to depose Muhammad Shiran Khalji, made Iwaz governor of Bengal; he gave the post up freely to Ali Mardan Khalji in 1210.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When the nobles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> executed the unpopular Ali Mardan in 1212, Iwaz was restored. He called back the nobles Ali Mardan had exiled and moved the capital from Devkot to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaur">Lakhnauti</a> (Gaur), which he rebuilt with three high ramparts and the river on its west, founding the fort-town of Basankot opposite it. Seeing that the rains and rivers made cavalry of little use, he raised infantry known as paiks and a boat fleet. He had the Friday khutbah read in his own name, invoked the Abbasid caliph Al-Nasir on his coins, and stressed his ties to the former Ghurid dynasty against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iltutmish">Iltutmish</a>, a sultan of the Mamluk dynasty.</p><p>He founded mosques, paid pensions to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulama">ulama</a> and invited preachers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> such as Jalaluddin bin Jamaluddin Ghaznavi. A khanqah built in Birbhum in 1221, while his son Ali Sher Khalji governed there, carries the earliest known stone inscription naming a Muslim ruler in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>. Iwaz made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vanga">Vanga</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirhut">Tirhut</a> and Utkala tributaries, and according to the historian Minhaj-i Siraj Juzjani he conquered Lakhnur; in 1214–15 he repelled an invasion from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Orissa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His hold on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> alarmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iltutmish">Iltutmish</a>. Delhi's army retook Bihar in 1225 and faced Iwaz at Teliagarhi, and by a treaty he agreed to pay 8 million takas and 38 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a> and to put Iltutmish's name on his coins and in the khutbah. Once the Delhi forces left he declared independence again and drove Alauddin Jani from Bihar. In 1227, while he was campaigning in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, Prince Nasiruddin Mahmud put down a rising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a> and marched on Bengal; Iwaz hurried back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaur">Lakhnauti</a> and was killed in the fighting there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Iwaz Khalji” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Iwaz Khalji</b>, who took the title Ghiyath al-Din, was the most effective ruler: he built roads and embankments, created a river fleet and struck coins in his own name.</blockquote><a href="/state/khalji-dynasty-of-bengal">Khalji dynasty of Bengal</a></li></ul></section>
